# `a[i] * b[i]`: a hole and a position past the end give `undefined`, and `undefined` is NaN for arithmetic with a number; so the read may give NaN itself and the product needs no question.
# That was said only where the other operand was built already and was a number. Of two reads the first found the second not built, and the second found the first `number | undefined`:
# both carried the mark of `undefined`, and the product asked each of them for it again: 4 of the 19 instructions an element of a dot product.
# Now the other operand may be a number or `undefined`, and of a read by key that is not built yet Link's word is asked: the same word that read is typed by, and stops at when it is false.
# It stays as it was for `+` beside what may be a text (joined): there `undefined` is written out.
# Beside a list of anything (quotients, both orders) and under the operators on bits (bits) NaN and `undefined` answer alike whatever stands beside them: a text, an object that logs, a big integer that throws.
# f1 = dot, f2 = differences, f3 = joined, f4 = quotients, f5 = bits, f6 = mixes, f7 = ratios
# -- source: two-elements-that-meet-in-arithmetic-both-read-a-hole-as-nan.ts
